  "step_2": "The individual reports reviewing Lottery A first, then B. They note that in both cases, one 'loses 2700 Euro'—this corresponds to the expected value of both lotteries. The individual states they were guided by 'the lower probability of losing anything at all'—referring to the 55% chance of losing nothing in B versus the 10% chance in A. This indicates the individual focused on the probability of avoiding a loss (i.e., getting 0 Euros) and preferred the option with the lower chance of incurring any loss. Their justification is based on minimizing the chance of a negative outcome, not on variance or dispersion of outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on variance, which reflects the spread of outcomes around the mean. While the individual acknowledges the same expected loss (-2700), they do not mention or imply consideration of outcome dispersion, squared deviations, or variability beyond the probability of incurring a loss. Instead, their justification centers on the probability of avoiding a loss altogether, which aligns more with a 'minimax regret' or 'probability of loss minimization' heuristic than variance maximization. Although the individual ends up choosing B—the same lottery preferred by the variance-based reason—their reasoning process does not reference any element of variance. The information considered (probability of loss) and the justification (avoiding loss) are distinct from the variance-based rule. Therefore, there is no evidence that variance was part of the deliberation.",
